    <string-array name="whats_new_0_3_0">
        <item>A reminder takes the whole screen only when nothing else is open: with an app in front of you it arrives as a banner instead, and the banner opens it.</item>
        <item>Putting one off: ten minutes, two hours, tomorrow at the same time, the weekend (Friday at 20:30, or whenever you say in Settings) and next week — as buttons a thumb cannot miss.</item>
        <item>A reminder no longer needs a trigger or an action. Text and a tag is enough: it is simply kept, under "kept, not timed".</item>
        <item>With more than one trigger you choose between "any of them" and "all of them" — all of them rings with the last to happen, and the card ticks off the ones already behind you.</item>
        <item>The editor reads as four parts, each in its own card with its own icon, and every edge you can press is finally visible.</item>
    </string-array>
    <string-array name="whats_new_0_3_1">
        <item>While a reminder has no "when" yet, the three answers people give most — in half an hour, tonight, tomorrow morning — are one tap away, before "Add a trigger".</item>
        <item>Swiping a card answers the moment it takes: the glyph springs and the colour deepens, on the same tick as the vibration.</item>
        <item>For a screen reader: sections are headings, day and action toggles say whether they are on, every trigger says its kind, and Done and Delete are actions on the card itself — a swipe is not something TalkBack can do.</item>
        <item>Empty screens have a face: an icon before the sentence.</item>
        <item>Done times follow the phone\'s 12/24-hour setting, the time wheels grow with a large font size, and the small grey notes in the editor are readable on the light theme too.</item>
    </string-array>
    <string-array name="whats_new_0_3_2">
        <item>"On" finally looks on. A chosen tag, action, preset or segment swaps ink and paper — the same inversion as the Save button — instead of turning a slightly lighter grey.</item>
        <item>A chosen day is a solid disc in the time colour, so a week reads as a pattern of lit and unlit.</item>
        <item>In the editor, each trigger row wears its family\'s colour: a date is blue, a place green, a chance violet, before a word is read.</item>
        <item>The editor\'s cards stand off the ground the way Home\'s do, and toggles turn over with a short fade rather than popping.</item>
    </string-array>
